-- title:
--   Pointwise products of two residue lists in the Fermat ring
-- statement:
--   Let $A, B$ be lists of $N$ canonical residues modulo $2^n+1$, $B$ on stack `XD` and $A$ on `YC`, with registers holding $N$, $2^n$, $2^n+1$, and let the multiplication spec $M$ multiply $(n+1)$-bit integers at cost $\le Φ_m$. For $W\ge 2n+2$, $W \ge \mathrm{zsz}(N)$, the program `pmL` replaces $B$ by the list of products `pmulZ (2^n+1) A B` $= (a_i b_i \bmod 2^n+1)_i$ and consumes $A$, within cost `pmCost N W Φm` $= N(Φ_m + 110000(W+2)) + N(14000(W+2)+30) + 8000(W+2)+64$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The machine layer: `IState HS` is a state with $64$ stacks of items (`Item` = integer or mark), an input position and an output; `RM` programs (straight-line register code `RS`, loops `rep`, conditionals, calls of the multiplication procedure through a `MulSpec` $M$ giving its domain and cost) have a functional semantics `p.run s`, a definedness predicate `p.ok M s` and a cost `p.cost M s`; `Spec M p s s' c` abbreviates `p.ok M s ∧ p.run s = s' ∧ p.cost M s ≤ c`. `ints L` / `gints U` push an integer list / a list of pairs as items (head = top); `zsz z` is the bit length of $|z|$. `MulBnd M m Φ` says the multiplication procedure accepts all pairs of integers of bit length $\le m$ at cost $\le Φ$. Register names (`GU`, `CR`, `KB`, `XN`, …) are fixed stack indices.
-- source:
--   D. Harvey, J. van der Hoeven, Integer multiplication in time O(n log n), Ann. of Math. 193 (2021), 563–617 (Theorem 1.1: multitape Turing machine multiplication in O(n log n)). Lemma `NLogN.FW.pmL_spec` of the Lean development NLogN/HFW7.lean (lines 180–253), written for the prove2.me integer-multiplication campaign.

